---
// Privacy policy for escapepod, the personal app that reads Google calendars.
// Google's OAuth consent screen needs a public policy URL to publish the app.
// Hidden like the style guide: noindex, nofollow, out of the sitemap, and
// linked from nowhere.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="Privacy policy for escapepod, a personal app."
  noindex={true}
>
  <header class="post-header">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="section-lede">For escapepod, a personal app I run for myself.</p>
  </header>

  <section>
    <p>
      escapepod is a planner I built and host for my own use. When a Google account is
      connected, it reads that account's calendar list and events, read-only, so they
      can show up next to my tasks. It never creates, changes or deletes anything in
      Google Calendar.
    </p>

    <p>
      Events and the access token are stored on my own server. The token is encrypted
      at rest. Nothing is shared, sold, used for advertising, or used to train anything,
      and the data isn't used for any purpose besides showing the calendar in the app.
    </p>

    <p>
      escapepod's use of information received from Google APIs adheres to the
      <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>,
      including the Limited Use requirements.
    </p>

    <p>
      Disconnecting an account in the app's settings deletes its stored events and token.
      Access can also be revoked at any time from
      <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions">your Google account's permissions page</a>.
    </p>

    <p>Questions go through the <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
